\begin{abstract}
A short skill document---a procedure, a lookup table, a worked example---placed
in a language model's context reliably improves accuracy. Where that improvement
lives inside the model is not known, and the standard place to look for it is
empty. On $55$ third-party clinical-calculator skills, every vector written into
the last prompt position lands on the no-document baseline: the content component
$\dvec = h_{\text{skill}} - h_{\text{wrong-skill}}$, the presence component
$\gvec$, donors from other instances and other skills, and replacing the entire
state with the with-document one---five arms, each at its best layer, all within
$0.10$ of a model given no document at all, against $0.95$ for the document
itself. The content is not absent from that position: it identifies which of $41$
skills is present at $0.93$--$1.00$ against a chance rate of $0.024$. What fails
is the channel. Holding the model, the document and the items fixed and changing
only the answer format, a single-position patch recovers $1.000$ of the rescued
items for a one-token answer, $0.30$ for a short number, and $\mathbf{0.000}$
when the same items are answered by reasoning step by step. One position carries
the model's decision, and a decision spread over hundreds of tokens does not fit.
We then find the channel that does carry: the document's own token span.
Substituting it, length-matched so no position changes its rotary phase, takes
the rescued items from $0.03$ to $1.00$. Because a length-matched receiver
already holds a document, that substitution \emph{is} content injection at
$\alpha=1$ spread over $m$ positions---so the whole vector battery becomes
available on real skills, and it behaves like a mechanism: a dose threshold
between $\alpha=0.5$ and $0.75$, no transfer across skills against a
position-matched control ($0.23$ vs $0.93$), collapse under permutation of the
span's own positions, and nothing from norm-matched noise. What carries is not a
common shift---averaging over every skill recovers $0.03$ while accounting for
$58\%$ of the vector's energy---and, with the document ahead of the question,
contains no task information at all: its span is bit-identical across a skill's
instances, and that task-free object is sufficient on its own.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A ``skill'' is a short document---a procedure, a formula, a lookup table, a
worked example---placed in a model's context so it can do a task it otherwise
cannot. The practice is now standard, and there is a benchmark literature that
measures its effect: how much accuracy a skill buys, which module of the
document matters, how the gain scales. Every one of those measurements is
behavioural. None of them says what the document does inside the model.

The natural way to look is to compare the model's internal state with and
without the document. We did, and the first thing we found was that this
comparison does not work in its obvious form. At the last prompt position, the
residual stream moves a long way when a skill document is added---and through
the layers in which the document is actually being read it moves almost exactly
as far, in almost the same direction, when the document is replaced by an
unrelated one of the same length. On our synthetic control the cosine between
the two displacements is $0.92$--$0.95$ from layer 0 to layer 10, peaking at
$0.9475$ (CI$_{95}$ $[0.9469, 0.9479]$) at layer 4. On a second task family, at
layer 10, two skills with mutually exclusive content sit at $0.9716$ while an
\emph{irrelevant} document sits at $0.9445$ and $0.9555$ from them---a
difference of $0.016$--$0.027$, tightly estimated and almost nothing. Read
naively, that says the injection carries little information about what the
document says. Read against behaviour, it cannot: on 1{,}100
clinical-calculator instances with third-party skills, the gold skill is worth
$+40.5$pp over no document, and a length-matched \emph{wrong} skill is worth
$-1.3$pp $[-7.6, +5.7]$---a clean zero. Behaviour is all content. Geometry, over
the layers that do the reading, is almost all presence.

